# /apps/afterframe was the app's live URL before it was renamed to Aftershot,
# and the canonical form carried a trailing slash. The route id is the content
# filename, so renaming the file moved the URL; without these, anyone holding
# the old link gets a 404. Permanent, because the move is.
#
# Both forms are raw rules here rather than split between this file and
# Astro's `redirects` config. Two reasons. The config cannot express a
# trailing-slash-only key at all — it collapses one onto the non-slash route —
# so the live URL could never have been covered there. And relying on the
# Cloudflare adapter to APPEND its generated rules onto this file is real but
# undocumented internal behaviour, pinned behind a caret range: a routine
# dependency bump could drop the redirect while the build still succeeded and
# every test still passed, and the only symptom would be the old URL quietly
# 404ing in production.
#
# This site deploys as a Cloudflare Worker, not Pages (see wrangler.jsonc:
# `main` is dist/_worker.js/index.js, with `assets.directory` pointing at
# dist/) — dist/_routes.json is a Pages artifact and plays no part here.
# Workers Static Assets reads and honours public/_redirects directly, and
# matches these as literal strings with no trailing-slash normalisation —
# which is why both forms need their own line. Verified under `wrangler dev`
# (the Worker runtime, not `wrangler pages dev`): both /apps/afterframe/ and
# /apps/afterframe 301 to the right place.
#
# scripts/verify-parity.sh asserts both rules survive into dist/_redirects.
/apps/afterframe/    /apps/aftershot/    301
/apps/afterframe     /apps/aftershot     301
